 physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n\nChronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm vs Plasma Cell Disorder:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n  Favours second: abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, elevated serum creatinine\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Plasma Cell Disorder:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n  Favours second: abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, decreased circulating antibody level\n\nRelated to Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts: cd4+/cd56+ hematodermic neoplasm, hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, mast cell neoplasm\n\nRelated to Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm: bone marrow cancer, chronic eosinophilic leukemia, chronic eosinophilic leukemia, not otherwise specified\n\nRelated to Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts: hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, lymphoid leukemia (disease), lymphoma\n\nRelated to Plasma Cell Disorder: hematological disease associated with an acquired peripheral neuropathy, myeloid neoplasm, non-secretory plasma cell myeloma\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue → fatigue → Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts\n  A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue → fatigue → Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts\n  A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue → fatigue → Plasma Cell Disorder"}
